course: dictionary
lesson: 2025
title: Secret or private?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you about keeping things hidden. Confidential is one of them. Look at the words around it to find its meaning. «Confidential means kept private; helplines and counsellors keep what you say private unless someone is in danger.» The clues are kept private, so confidential means secret or private.
explain: Confidential information is secret, and only certain people should see or share it. Your doctor keeps your records confidential, and so should your school. Ask yourself: who is allowed to know this? If only a few people are allowed to know, the word is confidential.
explain: The word can also describe a way of speaking. A confidential voice is quiet and private, like a friend telling you something just for you. The voice shows trust. To check your meaning, swap in "private" or "secret" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Confidential means secret or private.
fact: Confidential information should only be seen or shared by certain people.
fact: Passwords and personal details are confidential, so do not share them online.
fact: A quiet, trusting way of speaking can also be confidential: «in a confidential voice».
fact: Phrases to know: «strictly confidential» means very private, «keep confidential» means do not tell, and «in confidence» means as a secret.
exercise: Mum says her doctor keeps her records confidential. What does confidential mean here? | Written in a loud voice | Lost in the post | *Kept secret, seen only by certain people | Shared with everyone in the street
exercise: Sam whispers, «Keep this in confidence, please.» What does in confidence suggest? | *As a secret, not to be told | As a game of tag | As a shopping list | As a loud announcement
exercise: Find and copy the word that means secret or private: «The bank kept her account details confidential.» | account | details | bank | *confidential
test: «Strictly confidential» is written on a letter. What does this mean? | Very funny, so read it out loud | *Very private, only for the person named | Free to share with anyone | Written in pencil
test: A student spoke in a confidential voice to her friend. What does this show? | She was singing a song | She was reading a list of rules | She was angry and shouting | *She trusted her friend and wanted it kept private
exam: Passwords are confidential, so you should | *keep them private and never share them online | write them on a public sign | tell them to strangers | share them with your best friend
